### The two that were drawn, not chosen

Two of the original five replaced an upstream icon that was *semantically* right
and *visually* wrong, which is the only kind of substitution worth recording:

- `stop` was `delapouite/square`, which draws a **hollow** square. A stop button
  is a solid block in every transport control ever shipped, and the hollow version
  read as an outline next to the filled `play` triangle.
- `new_list` has no usable upstream glyph. `delapouite/files` (stacked pages)
  turns to mush at 20px, and the mark that shipped here is the eight-ray sunburst
  the old generator drew — 8 rays from 40% out to 90% of the radius, no core.

### The logo

`logo` is the app's own mark and was never an upstream icon: a lightning bolt over
a play triangle on a rounded plate. It briefly *was* — `lorc/double-quaver`, a
beamed pair of notes, which is what the SVG icon migration left behind and what
made the ☰ button stop looking like tplay. The artwork is back.

`themes/dark/icons/logo.svg` is traced from the original 776×776 render, so the
four silhouettes are exact. Its shading is **one linear gradient per shape**, not
the render's per-limb lighting, which is why it reads flatter than the raster did.
Tracing the shading bands too was measured at ~160 KB of path data, against ~12 KB
for this, so the gradient is the trade that was taken deliberately.

Two consequences for the rest of this file:

- It is **not** one of the two edits below — it has a `stroke` and a `<defs>` block,
  and it is not built from an upstream path. The "re-deriving" recipe does not
  apply to it.
- It is **not** per-theme and **not** recoloured. The three per-theme copies it
  replaces were byte-identical, so there is no palette token behind it;
  `recolor_icons.py` lists it in `FIXED` and leaves it alone. A theme that ships no
  logo of its own reaches the default theme's, the same as any other icon.

### Re-deriving a file

The upstream files are stored with two edits and nothing else: the
`<path d="M0 0h512v512H0z"/>` background rectangle that game-icons ships every
icon with is removed, and the remaining path's `fill` is replaced with the
theme's own palette token. The path data is otherwise byte-for-byte upstream, so
any icon can be re-fetched and re-checked with:

```
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/game-icons/icons/master/<author>/<name>.svg
```

A future version of game-icons.net may replace an icon's artwork under the same
name. The names above are the ones the 16 upstream shapes were taken from on
2026-09-27.

### The two edits matter

These are the two edits the 16 **upstream** files carry. The background rectangle
is load-bearing to remove, not cosmetic: it is an opaque black 512×512 fill, so an
icon that kept it rasterizes to a solid black square.
`every_icon_has_a_parseable_svg_source` in `tests/gui_tests.rs` asserts each file
has both an opaque and a transparent pixel, which is what catches it.

The `fill` is what makes each theme's set its own, and it is why the icons are
stored per theme rather than shared. Change a token in a `theme.json` and run
`python3 themes/recolor_icons.py` to propagate it. The logo is excluded from both
halves of that — see above.

## Test audio fixtures

The eight files in `tests/fixtures/` are one real recording encoded into eight
containers, taken from [audiojs/audio-lena](https://github.com/audiojs/audio-lena)
— "the audio equivalent of the Lena test image".

| | |
|---|---|
| Original | [freesound #246148](https://www.freesound.org/people/heshamwhite/sounds/246148/), *heshamwhite* |
| Licence | **CC0 1.0** — public domain dedication |
| Retrieved | 2026-09-27 |

CC0 requires no attribution and imposes no restriction, so these can sit in the
repository without making it the most restrictive thing in the tree. The
attribution is recorded anyway, because knowing a fixture's origin is the only
way to replace it safely. `tests/fixtures/README.md` has the per-file detail and
explains why the other 13 files of the upstream set are absent.

